Since we've started using a more recent version of language server, we've ran into issues with getting empty diagnostics. I pinned it down to a difference between the old version of a server and the new one. Windows has nothing to do with it.
TL;DR: we get empty diagnostics due to [this line](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-json-languageservice/blame/main/src/services/jsonValidation.ts#L36), which in turn is triggered in more recent server by [this more recent line that's included in the new version of a JSON server](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/main/extensions/json-language-features/server/src/jsonServer.ts#L240)
Obviously, this being JS, this has to have a bunch of papercuts :) In this case the intention was to fall back to `true` value if `validate` key is not set at all, but instead.. ah, damn, just see for yourself:
```
let settings = {json: {}}
let validateEnabled = !!settings.json?.validate?.enable;
console.log(validateEnabled)

let settings2 = {json: {validate: {enable: true}}}
let validateEnabled2 = !!settings2.json?.validate?.enable;
console.log(validateEnabled2)
```
Setting `validate` explicitly in our initialization options for the server fixes it. I'll bump Preview and nightly with it.
